HELLO.DOC - build and understand the HELLO.ASM 8086 DOS COM example

HELLO.DOC is a plain ASCII text file; read it with TYPE HELLO.DOC or MORE.
Work on a writable copy of the M19 native 2HD boot disk, not the release master.
Use 640 KiB of installed RAM for the tested small assembly workflow. A retained
640-KiB BIOS selection does not supply missing physical RAM. At 512 KiB
installed, the sample JWASMR build could not start on the tested VA/VA2 models.

From the A: prompt, make a working directory and copy the original source:

    MD WORK
    CD WORK
    COPY A:\HELLO.ASM HELLO.ASM

In that directory, build and run the COM program:

    JWASMR -0 -bin -Fo=HELLO.COM HELLO.ASM
    HELLO.COM

The -0 option selects 8086 instructions. -bin produces a raw binary file;
-Fo=HELLO.COM names the output. There is no linker step. Check JWASMR's exit
message before running HELLO.COM; do not run an executable left by an older
build if the assembly fails. To remove an old output first, use:

    IF EXIST HELLO.COM DEL HELLO.COM
    JWASMR -0 -bin -Fo=HELLO.COM HELLO.ASM

Run HELLO.COM only after JWASMR reports success. An existing output file alone
is not proof of a successful assembly; BUILD.BAT also checks ERRORLEVEL.

HELLO.ASM selects the tiny memory model and ORG 100h, so DOS can load the
binary as a COM program after its 256-byte program prefix. DOS INT 21h with
AH=09h prints the dollar-terminated message and AH=4Ch returns to the shell.
The example does not call an IBM-PC video, keyboard or disk BIOS, and it does
not need a numeric coprocessor. It is a small sample, not a guarantee that
larger source files fit the same RAM setting.

For the combined COM and MZ demonstration, copy MZDEMO.ASM and BUILD.BAT into
WORK too, then run BUILD. BUILD.BAT deletes both old outputs, assembles both
examples with JWASMR, runs the new programs, and checks DOS memory with
MEMMAP /CHECK. QUICKSTR.TXT explains optional EDLIN editing and its backup.
